## Shocking Statistics: A Growing Wave of Recalls

As we near the midpoint of 2026, the tally of food recalls has already reached 337, a concerning number that shows an upward trend compared to previous years. Among these, 91 are classified as Class I recalls, posing serious health risks. Class II recalls dominate the numbers, comprising 218 cases thus far, while Class III recalls total 28.

## What Happened Recently?

In the past couple of weeks, several noteworthy recalls have been issued, with May 6th being a particularly active day:

- *Hadson (Toko) Trading Co. Inc.* recalled Shrimp Negita Crackers due to undeclared FD&C Yellow 6.
- *Guangdong Zonegoing Food Co., LTD* issued a recall for various 52USA brand Popping Boba products (Passion Fruit, Mango, and Strawberry flavors) containing unapproved food colorings.
- *LACTALIS CANADA, INC* found glass pieces in their Specially SELECTED Vanilla Creme Brulee.
- *Schreiber Foods Inc* recalled Einstein Bros Bagels Honey Almond Double-Whipped Shmear due to undeclared almond allergens.
- *Hemu Trading Inc* issued a Class I recall for enoki mushrooms contaminated with Listeria monocytogenes.

## Repeat Offenders

While some brands make their appearance only occasionally, there are repeat offenders that raise eyebrows. *Moonlight Packing Corporation* and *Albertsons Companies LLC* lead the charge with their frequent Class I recalls. The former has earned a disturbing recall score of 96, marking it as one of our "worst offenders" of this year.

## Monthly Analysis

Looking at the monthly breakdown for the past 6 months, we notice a peak in October 2025 with 146 recalls, followed by a steady decline and then another rise in February 2026 with 102 recalls. May 2026 has registered 10 recalls so far, and while this number might seem low, the presence of Class I recalls in the mix is concerning.

## Verdict

Despite increased regulations and monitoring, food recalls continue to be a persistent issue. The data highlights flaws in manufacturing practices, quality control, and regulatory compliance across the industry. As consumers, it is paramount to stay informed about the brands we trust and the products we consume. 

## Call to Action

The year is far from over, and with recalls showing no signs of abating, it is vital that consumers demand accountability from producers. Whether it's strawberry Popping Boba or enoki mushrooms, every item on the shelf deserves rigorous scrutiny.

